# CODEOWNERS — ownership metadata. It says who to ask. It is NOT a merge gate.
#
# An earlier version of this comment quoted ci-cd.md's "one approval from the code owner"
# and called this file "the mechanism behind 'only Thomas merges'". That wording was
# SUPERSEDED before this branch was written: decision-log.md, 2026-09-06, "Merge governance
# — required approving reviews is zero, and that is deliberate" sets **required approving
# reviews = 0** and **Require review from Code Owners = off**, both deliberately, and says
# in terms that CODEOWNERS is ownership metadata rather than a mechanical gate. ci-cd.md
# § Branching and CLAUDE.md § How work reaches `main` both already read that way; this file
# was the last place still quoting the old sentence.
#
# Do NOT enable "Require review from Code Owners" to make this file mean more. The reason is
# recorded in that decision: a required approving review from a one-person team documents a
# gate rather than providing one, and the control that actually keeps a bad change out of
# `main` is the required independent review(s), the required Opus security review where in
# scope, and the required status checks — not an approval count.
#
# UPDATED 2026-09-15 (decision log, same date, "Governance reset"): merge execution is
# delegated to the orchestrating Claude session once every one of those gates is genuinely
# green on the exact candidate SHA. This supersedes "only Thomas merges" above and in
# ci-cd.md/agent-workflow.md/CLAUDE.md's older wording. Lane/subagents still never merge, and
# no bypass of any required check or review tier is authorized by this delegation.
#
# Agents produce branches, tested code and pull requests. They never self-approve
# (AGENTS.md do-not 7) and never merge except the orchestrating session, through the normal
# flow, once every gate above is satisfied (do-not 16).

